<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purnaiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Dewan Purnaiah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Purnaiah was a minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> as head of finance. After Tipu's death in 1799 the British made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> to the boy king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iii">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</a>, and he ran the restored state until 1811, rebuilding its finances and administration under British protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purnaiah.jpg" alt="Purnaiah" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Hickey, 1741-1824, Irish · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purniya,_Chief_Minister_of_Mysore.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
